            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Amy Howard"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Absolutely wonderful recipe! I did not have any sweet relish on hand but I did have bread and butter pickles. So I minced a few slices and used them instead. This recipe was so light and fresh tasting. I will NEVER use store bought tartar sauce again. One other thing...you really do need to let it set for awhile. I had to use mine almost immediately and while it was still very good, it definately improved with some time. Try this recipe, you will not be disappointed."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "The Cookworm"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Yum-o...Great basic tartar sauce. I've made it per instructed and with modifications. I prefer to use dehydrated \"Real-Lemon\" powder instead of lemon juice (it makes it less runny) and I double pickles (at least) but drain them on paper towel first, again so that it is not runny. I have also used horseradish instead of onion which gives it a different, but not unpleasant, taste. Leaving it to marry in the fridge is advised as it really brings the flavors together.",
            "datePublished": "2008-02-25T19:00:53.787Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Karen Saurer"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This is really the best tartar sauce....but I skip the optional lemon juice and we squeeze fresh lemon on our fish instead...not everyone likes the lemony taste in tartar sauce. I have enhanced this with a TBS. of ketchup or cocktail sauce and a tsp. of dried dill. This makes a yummy sauce for boiled shrimp or seared scallops.",
            "datePublished": "2007-05-10T05:41:08.700Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "CoolWheat1231"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This recipe does it all for tartar sauce. The lime or lemon juice is a must, not optional, use juice from the fresh fruit, not bottled. A little bit of hot pepper sauce in place of black pepper works well.",
            "datePublished": "2023-09-23T17:42:19.449Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "TaTaThomas"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Really quick and easy. I used dill relish because I don’t like sweet relish, but I did miss the sweetness that the bottled sauce has.. I’ll buy sweet relish just to make this again. \n\nTIP: You can substitute 1/8 teaspoon of onion powder if you don’t have minced onion or want to use the sauce immediately.",
            "datePublished": "2019-09-14T19:26:19.500Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "3"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Kaspian"
            },
            "reviewBody": "3 stars for a solid base recipe... but nowhere near a winning tartar sauce as written. Added to the 1 cu mayonnaise:\n1. 2 to 3 T sweet relish (not 1 T), squeeze out juice on paper towels.\n2. 1/2 a lemon's zest (no lemon juice)\n3. 1/4 to 1/2 t fresh Dill Weed (or Dill Weed herb paste sold in a tube in the produce section), to taste. \n(Salt + pepper to taste)\nDo these 3 things, and you'll never want to touch store- bought tartar sauce again.",
            "datePublished": "2017-05-08T04:18:35.873Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Betty Dickson"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This is a great recipe although I only used about a teaspoon of lemon juice and added a tsp of dijon for a little kick. My son and I did not like the tartar sauce my other half bought as it had dill in it. I made this and it is perfect. Grating the onion works super well.\nTonight I made this again and was out of dijon. I had some prepared horseradish. ie grated in a jar. I added a teaspoon of it instead. My other half found a mini food processor at a garage sale si I sliced some onion and processed it then added the mayo and horseradish and blended again then stirred in the sweet relish. Wow!",
            "datePublished": "2018-11-30T01:58:43.727Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Rhonda Spatt Bagdonas"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I’ve been using this recipe for a long time as my basic go to. I leave out the onion most times and add 1 extra tbsp of relish. I add 1-2 garlic cloves and start with about 1 tbsp of fresh squeezed lemon juice adding more if needed. I also add about 1/2 tsp of Old Bay seasoning. If you want to add a little spice…Cajun seasoning does the trick! I’ll never buy store bought again!",
            "datePublished": "2024-07-13T02:24:00.076Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "gothicmushroom"
            },
            "reviewBody": "As written the recipe is fantastic, and my review is based on the written recipe. But I'd like to share my changes: I do 1/4 c. mayo, and 3/4 cup whole milk plain Greek yogurt. This change takes the mayo aftertaste away and also makes this a bit better for you. You get a nice protein punch vs. just a fatty punch and it tastes virtually the same (without the mayo aftertaste).",
            "datePublished": "2020-08-21T21:34:39.210Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Hope AR"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Very good recipe. I add an entire lemon, juiced. Also, I add 2 Tbsp sweet relish and about 1 Tbsp water (I don’t like thick sauce). Never buy store bought anymore.",
            "datePublished": "2020-01-15T01:03:37.437Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Suzie"
            },
            "reviewBody": "We LOVE this tartar sauce! It's more like restaurant quality and taste than the jarred store bought tartar sauces. I quit using tartar sauce at home altogether after trying almost every one I could find on the shelves...with no luck. I have been using this one for several years now and SO happy I tried it! The only change I made is using dill relish instead of sweet relish. It's WONDERFUL!!!!",
            "datePublished": "2024-05-11T20:56:54.968Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Elizabeth Gower"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Love it. Will definitely be making it again!",
            "datePublished": "2025-08-06T04:38:16.229Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Melissa M"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Very nice tartar sauce, but the flavor was a little too mayonnaisey until I added a little garlic, horseradish and parsley. Still, it’s a good basic recipe which we’ll be using a lot.",
            "datePublished": "2025-05-27T19:35:45.857Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
